WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:04.444
Across months of care, a patient's
laboratory results arrive in fragments:

00:04.494 --> 00:07.586
different reports, different
dates, different formats.

00:07.790 --> 00:10.453
For a clinician investigating a complex case,

00:10.503 --> 00:15.155
following a subtle change across those
reports is slow, and easy to miss.

00:15.400 --> 00:20.360
LabSense AI helps clinicians investigate
complex cases and seek a second opinion

00:20.410 --> 00:24.292
by connecting laboratory findings
across reports and time.

00:24.490 --> 00:27.769
The first report asks the user
to confirm whose report it is.

00:27.819 --> 00:30.798
Every later report is checked against that answer.

00:30.950 --> 00:32.359
Each row is read as printed:

00:32.409 --> 00:35.121
the value, the unit, the
lab's own reference interval

00:35.171 --> 00:38.339
and the collection date, with
a link back to its source.

00:38.690 --> 00:42.602
LabSense brings these related
results into one investigation.

00:43.150 --> 00:46.281
Its leading assessment is a metabolic pattern.

00:46.331 --> 00:48.494
The supporting finding is HbA1c,

00:48.544 --> 00:53.774
above its printed interval on all
three reports, rising from 7.1 to 7.5.

00:54.030 --> 00:57.006
What limits it: symptom history
has not been collected,

00:57.056 --> 01:01.762
and a repeat fasting glucose or glucose
tolerance test would confirm the pattern.

01:02.310 --> 01:05.800
A second possibility stays open:
a kidney or urinary concern.

01:05.850 --> 01:11.090
Creatinine rose across all three reports,
and is above its interval in the latest one.

01:11.290 --> 01:16.647
A urine examination would help tell the possible
causes apart, and it is not available yet.

01:17.190 --> 01:19.997
Then it asks a question that
could change the picture.

01:20.200 --> 01:25.548
Has a doctor previously diagnosed
prediabetes, diabetes or metabolic syndrome?

01:26.500 --> 01:29.250
The answer is yes. LabSense uses it.

01:32.140 --> 01:36.083
The question about arranging a
urine test is answered "not sure".

01:36.280 --> 01:39.580
That answer is recorded, and
nothing is concluded from it.

01:40.130 --> 01:44.237
The leading assessment stays metabolic,
and the urgency stays at review.

01:44.440 --> 01:48.749
No single diagnosis is confirmed, and
the missing tests are still needed.

01:49.500 --> 01:51.660
Clinicians can inspect each step.

01:54.590 --> 01:59.381
A report printed for a different patient is
refused, and none of its values are stored.

01:59.580 --> 02:02.989
The review can be exported,
with its sources and answers.

02:03.640 --> 02:06.252
Today, LabSense is a working prototype.

02:06.450 --> 02:09.749
It has been tested using
historical laboratory records,

02:09.799 --> 02:14.494
including a 30-page multi-panel history,
and challenging synthetic cases.

02:14.700 --> 02:18.826
It supports diagnostic assessment,
while clinicians keep responsibility.

02:19.570 --> 02:24.484
We are targeting clinician-supervised
evaluation in December 2026.

02:24.690 --> 02:28.836
Later directions include connecting
to hospital records through FHIR,

02:28.886 --> 02:31.143
and adding ECG and wearable data.

02:31.890 --> 02:37.478
Our ambition is a longitudinal clinical intelligence
layer, starting with laboratory medicine.

02:37.830 --> 02:40.936
We welcome clinical partners for that evaluation.

02:41.880 --> 02:46.596
LabSense AI. Connected evidence for
better-informed clinical decisions.
